<h2>The statutory architecture</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Stage</th><th>Provision</th><th>Safeguard</th></tr>
<tr><td>Recording the statement</td><td>Section 26, POCSO Act</td><td>Assistance of a translator, interpreter or special educator where the child has a disability or does not share the language of the recorder; recording preferably by audio-video electronic means.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trial testimony</td><td>Section 38, POCSO Act</td><td>The Special Court takes the assistance of a qualified interpreter, expert or special educator when the child testifies.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Qualifications and panels</td><td>POCSO Rules, 2020</td><td>Districts maintain lists of qualified special educators and interpreters; their fees are payable by the State; support persons assist the child through the proceedings.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Competency</td><td>Section 124, BSA 2023</td><td>All persons are competent to testify unless prevented from understanding the questions or giving rational answers; the court tests the individual child, not the diagnosis.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Competency: the individual, not the label</h2>
<p>The law starts from competence, not from disability. A child with a hearing impairment who communicates in sign, a child with an intellectual disability who answers simply but rationally, a non-speaking child who writes or gestures — each is a competent witness if the court, after a preliminary assessment, is satisfied the child understands and responds rationally in her own mode of communication. Good practice is for the court to record that assessment: the questions asked, the manner of response, and the basis for finding the child competent. That record is what protects the conviction on appeal.</p>

<h2>Where prosecutions go wrong</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h3>No intermediary</h3><p>Statements taken from a disabled child without the special educator or interpreter the statute mandates invite the charge that the words on record are the recorder&rsquo;s, not the child&rsquo;s.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Supplied narratives</h3><p>Where a child cannot narrate conventionally, adults sometimes fill the gaps — names supplied by family, answers suggested by prosecutors. Tutoring destroys the very testimony the safeguards exist to preserve.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Unrecorded competency</h3><p>A conviction resting on a child&rsquo;s testimony with no visible competency assessment hands the defence its best appellate point.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Repeat examinations</h3><p>Calling the child again and again — investigation, Section 183 BNSS statement, deposition, recall — compounds trauma and breeds the inconsistencies that acquittals are built on.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Practice pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p><strong>For prosecutors and victim counsel:</strong> raise the disability at the first hearing and have a qualified special educator appointed from the district panel before any statement is recorded; insist on audio-video recording; brief the intermediary on neutrality — their task is communication, never suggestion; and place the child&rsquo;s disability certificate and communication profile on record so the court&rsquo;s accommodations are documented, not improvised.</p>
<p><strong>For defence counsel:</strong> the safeguards cut both ways — an accused is entitled to insist that the child&rsquo;s evidence reach the court through the statutory channel, and cross-examination through the judge, in simple language, is a right to be exercised with precision rather than waived through absence.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>The measure of a POCSO trial involving a disabled child is whether the process let the child tell her own story in her own way — with trained assistance, patience and a verified understanding of every question. The statute supplies the machinery; outcomes depend on whether the courtroom actually uses it.</p>
</div>
